143
00:07:27,970 --> 00:07:30,310
is where you'll observe a
proximal ulna that glides

144
00:07:30,310 --> 00:07:33,910
laterally easily when we
adduct the distal forearm.

145
00:07:36,010 --> 00:07:38,790
When evaluating a patient
with an elbow injury

146
00:07:38,790 --> 00:07:41,030
or elbow pain, we're
going to start with

147
00:07:41,030 --> 00:07:43,910
screening. We can screen
by observing the carrying

148
00:07:43,910 --> 00:07:46,730
angle of both arms and
comparing side to side.

149
00:07:46,730 --> 00:07:49,610
We can also assess for
restrictions in active

150
00:07:49,610 --> 00:07:52,830
range of motion of elbow
flexion and extension.

151
00:07:53,290 --> 00:07:57,730
To diagnose adduction and
abduction somatic dysfunctions

152
00:07:57,730 --> 00:08:00,090
of the ulno-humeral joint,
we are going to stand

153
00:08:00,090 --> 00:08:02,850
in front of the patient
who is seated at the table.

154
00:08:03,090 --> 00:08:07,590
We are going to monitor the
proximal ulna so that we

155
00:08:07,590 --> 00:08:11,730
can assess the medial and
lateral glide as we induce

156
00:08:11,730 --> 00:08:16,110
distal forearm AB duction
and AD duction. So the top

157
00:08:16,110 --> 00:08:20,210
image shows how we can
grasp the medial and lateral

158
00:08:20,210 --> 00:08:23,910
aspect of the proximal forearm
with both hands, and then

159
00:08:23,910 --> 00:08:26,350
we going to place the patient's
wrist and hand between

160
00:08:26,350 --> 00:08:30,370
our arm and side to support
it. The bottom image

161
00:08:30,370 --> 00:08:33,770
shows that we can grasp the
proximal forearm with one

162
00:08:33,770 --> 00:08:37,690
hand and then grasp the
patient's wrist or hand with

163
00:08:37,690 --> 00:08:40,350
the other hand. In both
circumstances we are going to

164
00:08:40,350 --> 00:08:45,030
want to have 5 to 45 degrees
of slight elbow flexion.

165
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:48,780
The motions that we are
going to introduce are

166
00:08:48,890 --> 00:08:52,250
distal forearm AB duction
and distal forearm AD

167
00:08:52,250 --> 00:08:56,210
duction. So in the top
right image, if we move the

168
00:08:56,210 --> 00:09:00,570
distal ulna laterally,
then we are going to assess

169
00:09:00,570 --> 00:09:04,330
or feel for the proximal
ulna moving medially.

170
00:09:06,370 --> 00:09:11,050
In distal forearm AD
duction, we're going to move

171
00:09:11,050 --> 00:09:15,230
the distal ulna medially
and we're going to assess

172
00:09:15,230 --> 00:09:19,190
for the proximal ulna
gliding or moving laterally.

173
00:09:24,490 --> 00:09:28,410
Now let's move on to the
radial head. So I have

174
00:09:28,410 --> 00:09:31,870
the color scheme here to
help keep straight which

175
00:09:31,870 --> 00:09:36,310
landmark is on which
bone. So for the radius,

176
00:09:36,310 --> 00:09:39,290
everything is in green. For
the humerus, everything is

177
00:09:39,290 --> 00:09:43,190
in blue. And for the ulna,
