178
00:09:43,550 --> 00:09:47,170
So the radial humeral
joint is between the

179
00:09:47,170 --> 00:09:51,030
radial head fovea, which
is shown in the top right

180
00:09:51,030 --> 00:09:55,110
image here and the
capitulum of the humerus.

181
00:09:55,670 --> 00:09:59,550
The radial ulnar joint
is between the radial

182
00:09:59,550 --> 00:10:03,030
head and the radial
notch of the ulna and the

183
00:10:03,030 --> 00:10:05,830
radial notch is shown in
that bottom right image.

184
00:10:06,330 --> 00:10:09,370
In each image we see
the annular ligament

185
00:10:09,370 --> 00:10:12,070
and it's shaped like
a tapered cup with the

186
00:10:12,070 --> 00:10:14,170
bottom broken out of
it and you can see that

187
00:10:14,170 --> 00:10:17,010
better on that bottom
right image. This prevents

188
00:10:17,010 --> 00:10:19,470
that distal displacement
of the radius.

189
00:10:22,450 --> 00:10:25,670
Now an example of when the
annular ligament does not

190
00:10:25,670 --> 00:10:28,050
prevent the distal
displacement of the radius is

191
00:10:28,050 --> 00:10:31,270
called nursemaid's elbow.
The mechanism of injury here

192
00:10:31,270 --> 00:10:34,730
is a sudden pull on an
extended and pronated arm.

193
00:10:35,010 --> 00:10:38,690
The most classic or common
example of how this occurs

194
00:10:38,690 --> 00:10:41,370
is shown in the image to
the right where you have

195
00:10:41,370 --> 00:10:44,850
an adult holding on to a
child's arm on each side and

196
00:10:44,850 --> 00:10:48,170
then lifting them into
the air. The child will

197
00:10:48,170 --> 00:10:51,230
experience pain because the
annular ligament becomes

198
00:10:51,230 --> 00:10:54,470
pinched. They'll hold their
arm very close to their body

199
00:10:54,470 --> 00:10:57,630
and have the elbow flexed
and the forearm pronated.

200
00:10:58,010 --> 00:11:01,950
Now to reduce this, it's
called a supination flexion

201
00:11:01,950 --> 00:11:04,030
technique. You're
going to start with the

202
00:11:04,030 --> 00:11:07,690
patient's arm in pronation
and get the elbow flexed

203
00:11:07,690 --> 00:11:10,230
to 90 degrees. With one
hand, you're going to apply

204
00:11:10,230 --> 00:11:13,830
pressure over the subluxed
or dislocated radial

205
00:11:13,830 --> 00:11:16,690
head. And then on the
other hand, you're going

206
00:11:16,690 --> 00:11:19,850
to supinate the forearm
and fully flex the elbow.

207
00:11:19,850 --> 00:11:23,390
This may result in a
click, pop, or clunk. Now

208
00:11:30,580 --> 00:11:33,320
the motions of the
radial head are due

209
00:11:33,320 --> 00:11:35,340
to a combination of
the humeral radial

210
00:11:35,340 --> 00:11:37,960
and proximal radial
ulnar joint motions.

211
00:11:38,380 --> 00:11:41,180
Radial head or radial
ulnar joint somatic

212
00:11:41,180 --> 00:11:43,920
dysfunction is a preference
of either anterior

213
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:47,260
or posterior glide
of the radial head.

214
00:11:48,110 --> 00:11:49,740
As you can see in
the image to the top

215
00:11:49,740 --> 00:11:52,700
right, when you have
forearm pronation,

216
00:11:52,700 --> 00:11:55,170
the radial head will
glide posteriorly,

217
00:11:55,170 --> 00:11:58,440
and with forearm
supination, you will

218
00:11:58,440 --> 00:12:00,710
have the radial head
glide anteriorly.

219
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:04,320
And so when we are
describing a posterior radial

220
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:06,480
head somatic dysfunction,
what we are saying is

221
00:12:06,480 --> 00:12:09,320
that as you pronate
the forearm, the radial

222
00:12:09,320 --> 00:12:13,780
head glides easily posteriorly,
but when you supinate

223
00:12:13,780 --> 00:12:17,180
the forearm, it resists
gliding anteriorly.

224
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:20,260
Now an anterior radial
head somatic dysfunction

225
00:12:20,260 --> 00:12:23,780
is where with forearm
supination, the radial

226
00:12:23,780 --> 00:12:27,760
head glides anteriorly
easily, but when you pronate

227
00:12:27,760 --> 00:12:30,840
the forearm, it resists
gliding posteriorly.

228
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:39,580
Now when evaluating
for a forearm injury or

229
00:12:39,580 --> 00:12:41,880
forearm pain, we're going
to start with screening.

230
00:12:42,120 --> 00:12:43,820
We're going to
screen by comparing

231
00:12:43,820 --> 00:12:46,220
side to side the
active range of motion

232
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:48,960
of forearm supination
and pronation.

233
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:51,940
Now to diagnose the radial
head we are going to be

234
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:54,060
standing in front of the
patient who is seated on

