261
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:29,740
head gliding posteriorly.
The image to the right

262
00:14:29,740 --> 00:14:32,780
shows an individual
falling backward where the

263
00:14:32,780 --> 00:14:36,680
impact forces the distal
radius posteriorly

264
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:40,980
which results in the
anterior radial head glide.

265
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:47,620
Now, to understand what
happens when we fall in

266
00:14:47,620 --> 00:14:49,740
an outstretched hand,
we need to understand a

267
00:14:49,740 --> 00:14:51,600
little bit more about the
interosseous membrane.

268
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:54,660
Now, within the
membrane, there is travel

269
00:14:54,660 --> 00:14:57,240
of blood vessels,
lymph vessels, and

270
00:14:57,240 --> 00:15:00,340
nerves. It also increases
the surface area

271
00:15:00,340 --> 00:15:02,880
for the deep forearm
muscles to attach to.

272
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:06,580
the fibers of the interosseous
membrane are primarily

273
00:15:06,580 --> 00:15:09,800
oriented from inferior
to medial when we're

274
00:15:09,800 --> 00:15:13,540
describing the direction from
the radius to the ulna so

275
00:15:13,540 --> 00:15:17,840
again inferior and medial
this allows the transfer

276
00:15:17,840 --> 00:15:21,400
of forces from the distal
radiocarpal or wrist joint

277
00:15:21,400 --> 00:15:25,700
up and through the ulna
and then into the humerus.

278
00:15:29,420 --> 00:15:32,380
Now let's move on to the
wrist joint. The wrist

279
00:15:32,380 --> 00:15:35,280
joint doesn't technically
include the ulna. The wrist

280
00:15:35,280 --> 00:15:38,060
joint is the articulation
between the distal

281
00:15:38,060 --> 00:15:40,580
radius and the radial ulnar
disc which you can see

282
00:15:40,580 --> 00:15:42,480
here in the bottom right
image. So you have the

283
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:46,180
distal radius and then the
radial ulnar articular disc

284
00:15:46,180 --> 00:15:50,880
and it's the articulation
to the scaphoid, lunate,

285
00:15:50,880 --> 00:15:55,000
and triquetrum of the
proximal carpals. And the

286
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,100
radiocarpal joint is a
condyloid synovial joint.

287
00:16:00,800 --> 00:16:04,300
The wrist joint is stabilized
by several ligaments.

288
00:16:04,300 --> 00:16:06,740
On the anterior and
posterior surface, it

289
00:16:06,740 --> 00:16:09,460
is stabilized by the palmar
and dorsal radiocarpal

290
00:16:09,460 --> 00:16:13,520
ligaments. So on the
left image, I mean, we

291
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:15,700
have the posterior view
of the right hand, and

292
00:16:15,700 --> 00:16:18,080
the dorsal radiocarpal
ligament is shown here.

293
00:16:18,440 --> 00:16:21,060
In the right image,
we have the anterior

294
00:16:21,060 --> 00:16:22,940
view of the right
hand, and we see

295
00:16:22,940 --> 00:16:25,380
the palmar radiocarpal
ligament here.

296
00:16:26,020 --> 00:16:29,220
The ulnar collateral
ligament is seen in the left

297
00:16:29,220 --> 00:16:32,320
image that travels from
the ulnar styloid process

298
00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:36,020
to the triquetrum, and
then the radial collateral

299
00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:39,540
ligament is also seen in
this left image where we go

300
00:16:39,540 --> 00:16:42,440
from the radial styloid
process to the scaphoid.

301
00:16:48,840 --> 00:16:51,260
I'm going to review the
distal radial ulnar joint a

302
00:16:51,260 --> 00:16:54,280
little further. The distal
radial ulnar joint is

303
00:16:54,280 --> 00:16:58,380
stabilized by a complex
referred to as the TFCC. It is

304
00:16:58,380 --> 00:17:01,380
composed of the articular
disc, which is seen here,

305
00:17:01,900 --> 00:17:05,740
the dorsal and ventral
radial ulnar ligaments.

306
00:17:05,740 --> 00:17:08,720
Those are shown here,
the dorsal radial ulnar

307
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:12,740
ligament, the dorsal
and ventral ulnocarpinal

308
00:17:12,740 --> 00:17:16,420
ligaments, and the floor of
the extensor carpi ulnarius,

309
00:17:16,420 --> 00:17:19,280
or ECU, tendon sheath,
which is shown here.

310
00:17:21,980 --> 00:17:25,160
let's move back to the
radiocarpal joint or wrist joint

311
00:17:25,160 --> 00:17:27,360
and talk about somatic
dysfunction of the wrist which

312
00:17:27,360 --> 00:17:30,500
is not related to gross
motions of the wrist but rather

313
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:34,360
small gliding motions of
the proximal carpals and

314
00:17:34,360 --> 00:17:36,680
these somatic dysfunctions
are often a combination of

315
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:40,740
these glides so when we have
wrist extension what the

316
00:17:40,740 --> 00:17:44,600
proximal carpals will do is
glide anteriorly With wrist

317
00:17:44,600 --> 00:17:48,980
flexion, the proximal carpels
will glide posteriorly.

318
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:53,780
With wrist adduction or
ulnar deviation, the proximal

319
00:17:53,780 --> 00:17:57,720
carpels will glide laterally.
And with abduction or

320
00:17:57,720 --> 00:18:00,400
radial deviation, the
proximal carpels will glide

321
00:18:00,400 --> 00:18:04,420
medially. So the somatic
dysfunctions are named for

322
00:18:04,420 --> 00:18:07,220
that preference. So for
extension somatic dysfunctions,

323
00:18:07,220 --> 00:18:10,420
the proximal carpels
prefer to glide anteriorly

324
00:18:10,420 --> 00:18:13,800
with wrist extension and
resist gliding posteriorly with

325
00:18:13,800 --> 00:18:16,960
wrist flexion. For a
flexion somatic dysfunction,

326
00:18:17,040 --> 00:18:19,380
the proximal carpals
prefer to glide

327
00:18:19,380 --> 00:18:22,440
posteriorly with wrist
flexion and resist

328
00:18:22,440 --> 00:18:24,920
gliding anteriorly
with wrist extension.

329
00:18:25,060 --> 00:18:28,640
For adduction or ulnar
deviation somatic

330
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:31,620
dysfunction, you'll find
that the proximal carpals

331
00:18:31,620 --> 00:18:34,480
prefer gliding laterally
with wrist ulnar

332
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:38,140
deviation or adduction and
resist gliding medially

333
00:18:38,140 --> 00:18:41,080
with wrist radial
deviation or abduction.

334
00:18:41,280 --> 00:18:43,660
For an abduction or radial

335
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:45,400
deviation somatic dysfunction,

336
00:18:45,540 --> 00:18:48,480
the proximal carpals will
prefer to glide medially

337
00:18:48,770 --> 00:18:51,320
with wrist radial
deviation or abduction

338
00:18:51,320 --> 00:18:53,980
but resist gliding
laterally with

339
00:18:53,980 --> 00:18:56,460
wrist ulnar deviation
or adduction.

340
00:19:11,730 --> 00:19:14,530
Evaluating a patient with
wrist injury or wrist

341
00:19:14,530 --> 00:19:17,370
pain, we're going to
start with screening and

342
00:19:17,370 --> 00:19:19,870
the screening is going
to be assessing active

343
00:19:19,870 --> 00:19:22,690
range of motion with the
various wrist motions

344
00:19:22,690 --> 00:19:25,310
and comparing side to
side for any restrictions.

345
00:19:25,920 --> 00:19:28,710
For diagnosing, we're going
to stand in front of the

346
00:19:28,710 --> 00:19:31,270
patient who is seated at
the table. The patient is

347
00:19:31,270 --> 00:19:33,380
going to have their arms
comfortably at their sides,

348
00:19:33,400 --> 00:19:35,860
and their elbows are going
to be flexed to 90 degrees.

349
00:19:36,110 --> 00:19:38,470
And then we're going to
test the following motions,

350
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:41,530
but I want to make a note
that ulnar and radial

351
00:19:41,530 --> 00:19:44,410
deviation restrictions
may differ if the forearm

352
00:19:44,410 --> 00:19:47,270
is pronated or supinated,
and that's due to the

353
00:19:47,270 --> 00:19:49,440
reciprocal motion of
the ulno-humeral and

354
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:51,810
radiocarpal joints that
we'll get to in a few slides.

355
00:19:51,930 --> 00:19:54,190
However, restriction
in wrist flexion

356
00:19:54,190 --> 00:19:55,710
and extension
doesn't really differ

357
00:19:55,710 --> 00:19:58,390
with forearm
supinated or pronated.

358
00:19:58,610 --> 00:20:01,430
So we're going to start
at the top right and

359
00:20:01,430 --> 00:20:03,870
the left image in the
top right and we're going

360
00:20:03,870 --> 00:20:06,030
to introduce wrist
extension and we're going to

361
00:20:06,030 --> 00:20:09,350
feel for proximal carpals
gliding anteriorly.

362
00:20:10,420 --> 00:20:12,370
And then the next
image, we're going to

363
00:20:12,370 --> 00:20:15,490
introduce wrist flexion
and we're going to feel

364
00:20:15,490 --> 00:20:18,070
for proximal carpals
gliding posteriorly.

365
00:20:19,370 --> 00:20:23,090
For the next picture in
the middle on the far

366
00:20:23,090 --> 00:20:25,610
right, we're going to
introduce wrist ulnar

367
00:20:25,610 --> 00:20:29,290
deviation or AD duction
and we're going to feel

368
00:20:29,290 --> 00:20:31,770
for the proximal carpals
gliding laterally.

369
00:20:32,950 --> 00:20:35,410
The final picture,
the bottom right,

370
00:20:35,410 --> 00:20:37,170
we're going to
introduce wrist radial

371
00:20:37,170 --> 00:20:40,350
deviation or AB duction
and we're going to

372
00:20:40,350 --> 00:20:42,970
feel for proximal
carpals gliding medially.

373
00:20:43,450 --> 00:20:46,430
Now, we can also just
directly assess the

374
00:20:46,430 --> 00:20:48,890
individual carpal bone
glides in order to diagnose

375
00:20:48,890 --> 00:20:51,930
specific carpal diagnoses,
such as scaphoid,

376
00:20:51,930 --> 00:20:54,150
lunate, or traquetrium
somatic dysfunctions.

377
00:20:54,550 --> 00:20:57,510
But this is the summary of
